WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.220
Film Courage: Steve in an interview with the
Sundance Institute you said you believe there

00:00:04.220 --> 00:00:11.509
is no financial incentive to draw the documentary
process out past a year but obviously this

00:00:11.509 --> 00:00:17.760
[film&nbsp;CRIME + PUNISHMENT]&nbsp;took four years
to make. I’m wondering how are you able

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:22.820
to put in four years of time and not run out?
Or did you run out of money several times

00:00:22.820 --> 00:00:24.039
and have to apply for grants?

00:00:24.039 --> 00:00:28.859
Stephen Maing, filmmaker of&nbsp;CRIME + PUNISHMENT:&nbsp;Yes,
I think most people working in non-fiction

00:00:28.859 --> 00:00:33.580
are finding themselves in situations that
are shooting for years in order to prove to

00:00:33.580 --> 00:00:40.590
funders that they have a credible story or
kind of like a proof of concept on hand. You

00:00:40.590 --> 00:00:48.450
hear it many times, you submit grant applications
or to funders and they’ll say&nbsp;Well, come

00:00:48.450 --> 00:00:53.290
back when you have more, this looks really
interesting&nbsp;to&nbsp;the point where people want

00:00:53.290 --> 00:01:03.740
rough cuts of work. It’s really a difficult
path to finish an independent documentary

00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:10.150
because you are expending all of your personal
savings or resources. Everybody is accruing

00:01:10.150 --> 00:01:14.610
(or most people are accruing) massive amounts
of personal debt on credit cards and what

00:01:14.610 --> 00:01:19.710
not and so it’s very hard to justify.

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:26.920
But yet when you feel like you are finding
yourself in the crux of a kind of, perhaps

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:34.990
never-before-seen story or access to individuals
who have not had their stories told, that’s

00:01:34.990 --> 00:01:47.890
a compelling reason to find&nbsp;any&nbsp;reason to
continue shooting. Luckily to be able to freelance

00:01:47.890 --> 00:02:01.020
in film for other projects was thankfully
a way to sustain myself when times were tight.

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:09.550
And then ultimately I was able to get funding
thankfully from these really amazing organizations

00:02:09.550 --> 00:02:09.979
and individuals.

